  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Let&apos;s Learn Together ( Apprendre tous Ensemble)</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">(Democratic Republic of the Congo):  Congolese children and youth, particularly the most marginalized, face multiple barriers to acquiring foundational skills, such as reading, literacy, and social and emotional development. Despite these barriers, Congolese value education and, when resources and education opportunities are available, families prioritize education...Similar to USAID&apos;s We&apos;re Reading, but implemented in different geographic zones, with FY 2022 funds, Let&apos;s Learn Together will leverage USAID&apos;s prior experience in supporting improved reading, literacy, and social and emotional learning outcomes. It will ensure that children aged six to nine in select marginalized and underserved zones in equateur, Maniema, Tanganyika, and Kinshasa gain foundational literacy and social and emotional skills, enabling them to function in school at a second grade level or higher. USAID will achieve results by engaging community leaders and parents and providing safe spaces, contextually-relevant materials, and multiple learning pathways for children who have had limited access to formal schooling, especially girls, indigenous children, and children with disabilities. Key adaptation and learning events will be identified throughout the life of the activity in order to identify opportunities for revision and forward planning...Let&apos;s Learn Together will advance the Integrated Country Strategy via Mission Objective 3.2 (Improve public institutions&apos; responsiveness, respect for human rights and basic freedoms, and accountability, including through strengthening education overall) by improving education services delivery, increasing access to education services, and strengthening partnership between education actors. It addresses the Administration policy priority of diversity, equity, and inclusion by targeting marginalized groups, including girls, indigenous children, and children with disabilities. ...</narrative>
  </description>
